      "title": "File:-conservationlands15 Social Media Takeover, August 15th, Santa Rosa and San Jacinto National Monument in California (20402146310).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/-conservationlands15_Social_Media_Takeover%2C_August_15th%2C_Santa_Rosa_and_San_Jacinto_National_Monument_in_California_%2820402146310%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:-conservationlands15_Social_Media_Takeover,_August_15th,_Santa_Rosa_and_San_Jacinto_National_Monument_in_California_(20402146310).jpg",
      "width": 3000,
      "height": 2096,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "mypubliclands",
      "description": "Welcome to the August #conservationlands15 Social Media Takeover! Today’s 15th posts feature urban escapes - BLM National Conservation Lands close enough to home for everyday getaways, like the Santa Rosa and San Jacinto National Monument in California!\nRising abruptly from the desert floor near sea level, the Santa Rosa and San Jacinto Mountains National Monument reaches an elevation of 10,834 feet on Mount San Jacinto. The monument provides a picturesque backdrop to local desert communities including Palm Springs. Visitors can enjoy magnificent palm oases, snow-capped mountains, a national scenic trail, and wilderness areas. The area’s extensive trail network can be accessed from both the Coachella Valley and the alpine village of Idyllwild. Hikers can choose from over 20 trails, with some trailheads accessible right from town. The area has always been a city escape from Los Angeles, less than 2 hours to the west. The Palms to Pines Scenic Byway climbs from the valley floor to some of the highest reaches of the monument with numerous scenic viewpoints along the way. Don’t forget to use the new recreational map to find your way around the area.\n\nPhoto by Bob Wick",
